      "source_example_id": "e-9909",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values",
      "kp_id": "e-9909"
    },
    "q-139533": {
      "dom_id": "step-q139533",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse.",
      "worked_solution": "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={6}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={6}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
