    "q-139554": {
      "dom_id": "step-q139554",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14225355/assets/63d5de8c08559ecc88b4fd7e2184204a1e6ea2f8a9e8ca6af7959e2354876e3f.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $9$ of ${A}^{T}A?$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=5={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={5}^{2}=25,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=3={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={3}^{2}=9.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=9$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
